# Copy to $SJEL_PERSONAL_ROOT/config/graphify.env and fill in for real. Nothing here is
# read unless that overlay file exists (tools/graphify.sh defaults to GRAPHIFY_BACKEND=omlx
# with no config at all). See tools/graphify.sh.

# GRAPHIFY_BACKENDS is an ordered preference chain. The first candidate that has a key AND answers
# a probe wins; each one that does not is skipped with a named reason on stderr, so "which backend
# did this actually run on" never needs a code read. `--check` prints the chain and the winner
# without spending a request.
#
#   GRAPHIFY_BACKENDS="nvidia opencode-go"   free tier first, paid fallback behind it
#   GRAPHIFY_BACKENDS="omlx"                 local only
#   GRAPHIFY_BACKENDS="nvidia none"          try the free tier, then explicitly stop
#
# `none` is a decision, not a fallthrough: it stops the walk where it sits, so "I chose code-only"
# and "nothing answered" report differently. GRAPHIFY_BACKEND (singular) still works and is read as
# a one-element chain, so no existing config has to change.
GRAPHIFY_BACKENDS="nvidia opencode-go"

# ENDPOINTS AND KEYS ARE NOT SET HERE. Every backend is declared once in the overlay's
# config/inference.json — base_url, model, and the file holding its key — and this wrapper reads
# that registry. libs/inference reads the same one, so the two cannot drift about where a key
# lives, which they would the first time one moved. Adding a provider is a registry entry, not
# another branch in tools/graphify.sh.
#
# Keys come from Vaultwarden through the one bridge that exists for it:
#
#   tools/materialize-inference-key <gemini|nvidia-nim|opencode-go>
#
# That writes config/runtime-secrets/<item> at mode 0600 straight from `bw get notes`. The bytes
# are never printed, never interpolated into a URL, and never passed as an argv element — this
# wrapper feeds the Authorization header to curl over stdin for the same reason.

# CHECK WHAT THE SEMANTIC PASS ACTUALLY CONTRIBUTES BEFORE PAYING FOR IT. It can fail while
# the command succeeds: graphify keeps the AST graph and exits 0 either way, so the only
# honest evidence is the artifact. `tools/graphify.sh` now says which of code-only or
# semantic it produced; to check an existing graph directly:
#
#   jq '[.nodes[], .links[]? | select(._origin != "ast")] | length' graphify-out/graph.json
#   jq '.hyperedges | length' graphify-out/graph.json
#
# Both zero means every element came from the AST pass and the backend contributed nothing.
#
# Measured here 2026-08-04, gemma-4-26b-a4b-it-4bit on a local oMLX server: both were zero
# across 6,665 nodes and 13,070 edges.
#
# THE FENCE IS NOT THE CAUSE. This note used to say graphify rejects the model's ```json fence.
# It does not: graphify 0.9.19 strips fences before parsing (llm.py `_parse_llm_json`), and it
# falls back to scanning for the first balanced {...} when that fails. Corrected 2026-08-06
# after reading that function and re-measuring; the fence is simply what shows up in the first
# 200 characters graphify prints, which is how it got blamed.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS, reproduced twice on a two-file corpus:
#   1. The model emits ~850 output tokens of pretty-printed JSON and stops MID-OBJECT.
#   2. Both parse strategies then fail — there is no closing fence and the braces never
#      balance, so the balanced-object scanner finds no complete object either.
#   3. graphify sees non-empty content with no nodes, calls it "hollow", and INFERS truncation.
#      Its "truncated at max_completion_tokens" line is that inference, not something the
#      server reported. Do not read it as a cap being hit.
#
# The cap is not the constraint: graphify requests 16384 output tokens for this backend, and a
# direct probe confirmed oMLX honours both max_tokens and max_completion_tokens well past 850.
# The same model, given the same instruction in a SHORT prompt, returns valid unfenced JSON that
# parses. So the stop is deterministic model behaviour on graphify's full extraction prompt, and
# the honest next step is a different local extraction model rather than another knob here.
#
# `--token-budget` (per-chunk INPUT cap, default 60000) is not it either: the reproduction ran on
# a ~60-word README, far under any chunk boundary, and still failed.
#
# THE TWO HALVES FAIL INDEPENDENTLY, so judge them separately. Community labelling uses the
# same backend and works fine here: `graphify label .` turned 261 of 468 `Community N`
# placeholders into prose like "Google Event Sync Logic" and "Timezone and Calendar Logic",
# 453 of 470 on the same model that cannot get an extraction chunk parsed.
#
# Labelling needs graphify-out/.graphify_analysis.json to do that. Without it the pass still
# reports success and still names every community — from each community's dominant node, so
# the output is filenames and two neighbouring communities can both come out as `main.rs`.
# A graph.json copied somewhere on its own therefore cannot be used to judge whether the
# backend works; that mistake produced a confident wrong answer here before the full-repo
# run contradicted it.

# SIZE THE MODEL TO THE MACHINE FIRST. Everything above documents one investigation that went
# through three wrong causes before landing, and the shape of that mistake is worth more than any
# individual finding: each wrong answer came from a corpus small enough to hide the real failure.
#
# Chunk budget, measured 2026-08-06. graphify chunks at 60,000 INPUT tokens by default, a
# cloud-sized number. A local server declaring a 32,768-token window rejects every such chunk
# with `exceeded context (BadRequestError)` before the model reads a token. tools/graphify.sh now
# derives the budget from the server's own declared window instead: one window holds the system
# prompt, the chunk AND the response, so budget = window - output - prompt reserve.
# `tools/graphify.sh --check` prints the resolved numbers.
#
# GRAPHIFY_TOKEN_BUDGET=20480       # per-chunk INPUT cap; default: fitted to the window
# GRAPHIFY_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192   # honoured by graphify itself; the reserve the fit assumes
#
# THAT FIT IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT, which is the part worth carrying forward. It removed
# the depth-0 wall and roughly tripled the chunk count, and the same full-corpus run still failed:
# 31 hollow responses, 8 rejections reappearing at deeper bisections, 4 empty-choices aborts.
# Mid-object stops landed at 851 / 1359 / 1775 / 2476 / 3483 output tokens, none near the 8192
# cap. A cap gives the same number every time; a 4x spread is a resource-driven abort.
#
# On a 24 GB machine running an `aggressive` memory guard against a 26B model there is no chunk
# size that leaves room for a useful extraction prompt and its response. The fix is a smaller
# model or more RAM, not another knob — which is what Axon#151 said before two later notes talked
# themselves out of it. A deployment that concludes this sets GRAPHIFY_BACKEND=none and keeps the
# AST graph, which is unaffected and complete.

# --- oMLX (local, Apple Silicon, default) ---
# The script maps this to graphify's `--backend openai`, defaults to the same local server as
# Comms, and reads `.auth.api_key` directly from oMLX's settings file. No key is copied into
# this overlay. Override only when this machine uses a non-standard oMLX location or alias.
OMLX_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1
OMLX_MODEL=gemma-4-26b-a4b-it-4bit
# OMLX_SETTINGS_PATH=~/.omlx/settings.json
#
# `tools/graphify.sh --check` validates the resolved endpoint/model contract without running
# extraction or uv.

# --- Ollama (compatibility only) ---
# Existing installations can still opt in explicitly:
# GRAPHIFY_BACKEND=ollama
# GRAPHIFY_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b

# --- nvidia (free NIM tier, cloud) ---
# Also mapped onto graphify's `--backend openai`. OPENAI_API_KEY is a REAL secret -- never
# put the actual key in this file (this is the checked-in *example*) or anywhere in Axon.
# `tools/setup-secret.sh` doesn't apply here -- it only provisions capabilities/<name>/
# service.toml-backed secrets, and graphify is a tools/ script, not a capability. Store the
# key in Vaultwarden yourself (the `bw` CLI, same store, just not through that capability-
# scoped wrapper) and paste the one required plaintext copy into your own copy of THIS file
# at $SJEL_PERSONAL_ROOT/config/graphify.env -- never in this checked-in example.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1
# OPENAI_MODEL=<a model id from https://build.nvidia.com/models>
# OPENAI_API_KEY=nvapi-...
